XI. Den store oversvømmelse
XI. The flood

Gilgamesh sagde til Utnapishtim, ham der boede langt borte: »Jeg står og ser på dig, Utnapishtim. Der er intet fremmed ved dit udseende. Du ligner mig, du er ikke anderledes. Du ligner mig. Du har mod på at kæmpe, og du lægger dig på ryggen for at hvile [?]. Sig mig, hvordan kunne du stå i gudernes forsamling og bede om evigt liv?«
Gilgamesh said to Utnapishtim, the one who lived far away, “I'm looking at you, Utnapishtim. There's nothing strange about your appearance. You look like me, you're no different. You look like me. You have the stomach for a fight, and you lie on your back to rest [?]. Tell me, how could you stand in the assembly of the gods and ask for everlasting life?”

Utnapishtim svarede Gilgamesh: »Jeg vil afsløre en hemmelig historie for dig, Gilgamesh. Jeg vil fortælle dig, hvad guderne besluttede. Du kender byen Shuruppak. Den ligger ved Eufrat. Byen var gammel, og guderne boede midt i den. De store guder besluttede at sende en oversvømmelse. Dér var Anu, deres far, krigeren Enlil, deres rådgiver, Ninurta, deres budbringer, og Ennugi, deres anfører [?]. Ninigi-azag, det er Ea, var selv med i deres råd. Men han røbede deres beslutning for en sivhytte:
Utnapishtim answered Gilgamesh, “I'll reveal a hidden story to you, Gilgamesh. I'll tell you what the gods decided. You know the city of Shuruppak. It stands on the Euphrates. The city was old and the gods lived at its heart. The great gods decided to send a flood. There were Anu their father, the warrior Enlil their adviser, Ninurta their herald and Ennugi their leader [?]. Ninigi-azag, that is Ea, took part in their council. But he betrayed their decision to a reed hut.

‘Sivhytte, sivhytte! Væg, væg! Hør efter, sivhytte, mærk dig det, væg! Du menneske fra Shuruppak, Ubara-Tutus søn: Riv dit hus ned og byg et skib. Opgiv dine ejendele og søg livet. Lad dine skatte ligge og red liv. Tag alle slags levende væsner med om bord. Det skib, du bygger, skal have de rigtige mål. Bredde og længde skal svare til hinanden. Sæt det så i søen.’ Jeg forstod ham. Jeg sagde til min herre Ea: ‘Herre, jeg bøjer mig for dine ord og gør, som du siger. Men hvad skal jeg sige til byen, til folk og til de ældste?’
“‘Reed hut, reed hut! Wall, wall! Listen, reed hut, pay attention, wall! You mortal from Shuruppak, son of Ubara-Tutu: pull down your house and build a ship. Give up your possessions and seek life. Leave your treasure and save life. Take all kinds of living things aboard. Give the ship you build the proper measurements. Its breadth and length must correspond. Then launch it on the deep.’ I understood him. I said to Ea my lord, ‘Lord, I honour your words and I'll do as you say. But what am I to tell the city, the people and the elders?’

Ea svarede mig, sin tjener: ‘Menneske, det her skal du sige: Enlil hader mig så meget, at jeg ikke længere kan blive boende i jeres by eller vende ansigtet mod det land, der hører Enlil til. Jeg tager ned i dybet for at bo hos min herre Ea. Men over jer vil han lade rigdom regne ned, fugle i mængde, en rig fangst af fisk […] og en stor høst […] en rigelig regn skal falde over jer [?].’
“Ea answered me, his servant, ‘Mortal, this is what you're to say: Enlil hates me so much that I can no longer live in your city or turn my face towards the land that belongs to him. I am going down to the deep to live with my lord Ea. But on you he will shower abundance: birds in profusion, a rich catch of fish […] and a mighty harvest […] plentiful rain shall fall on you [?].’

Da morgenen gryede […] [Fem beskadigede linjer.] Børnene kom med beg, og de stærke kom med alt det øvrige, vi skulle bruge. På femtedagen tegnede jeg skibets omrids op. Hver side var ti stangmål høj, og dækket målte tilsvarende ti stangmål. Jeg afsatte og byggede stævnen [?]. Seks gange afstivede jeg skibet på tværs. Jeg delte det i syv […] og inddelte det indvendige i ni. Jeg hamrede tætningen på plads og skaffede en stang. Alt, hvad der skulle til, kom jeg i. Jeg smurte seks shar asfalt på skroget og tre shar beg på indersiden. De kom med tre shar fedt i beholdere. Af det satte jeg én shar til side til rigningen [?]. Færgemanden stuvede de to øvrige shar af vejen. Jeg slagtede okser til […] og hver dag slagtede jeg lam. Arbejderne fik mjød, øl, olie og vin, som var det flodvand. De holdt fest som ved nytår.
“When morning dawned […] [Five damaged lines.] The children brought pitch and the strong brought everything else we needed. On the fifth day I marked out the ship's shape. Each side was ten rods high and the deck measured ten rods to match. I marked out and built the forepart [?]. Six times I braced her across. I divided her in seven […] and divided the interior in nine. I hammered the caulking into place and found a pole. Everything needed I put in. I smeared six shar of bitumen on the hull and three shar of pitch inside. They brought three shar of grease in containers. Of this I set one shar aside for the tackle [?]. The boatman stowed the other two shar away. I slaughtered oxen for […] and every day I slaughtered lambs. The workers had mead, beer, oil and wine as if it were river water. They feasted as at New Year.

[Fem brudte linjer nævner salve til hænderne, det færdige skib, den store Shamash, en vanskelighed, noget der føres op og ned, og »to tredjedele af det«.] Alt, hvad jeg ejede, lastede jeg om bord. Alt mit sølv lastede jeg om bord, alt mit guld lastede jeg om bord. Af alt levende tog jeg det med, som nyt liv kan komme af. Hele min slægt og familie gik om bord med mig. Kvæg og markens vilde dyr tog jeg med, og alle slags håndværkere.
“[Five broken lines mention salve for hands, the finished ship, great Shamash, a difficulty, something brought above and below, and ‘two thirds of it.’] Everything I owned I loaded aboard. All my silver I loaded aboard, all my gold I loaded aboard. Of every living thing I brought aboard the seed of life. All my relatives and family came aboard with me. I took cattle, wild animals of the field and craftspeople of every kind.

Shamash fastsatte timen: ‘[…] om natten skal det regne i strømme […]. Gå da ind i skibet og luk straks lugen.’ Timen kom […] om natten regnede det i strømme […]. Jeg så ud i dagen. Dagen var forfærdelig at se på. Jeg gik ind i skibet og lukkede straks lugen. For at skibet kunne lukkes, gav jeg færgemanden Puzur-Amurri [?] agterstavnen med dens udstyr.
“Shamash named the hour. ‘[…] at night rain will pour down […]. Enter the ship then and shut the hatch at once.’ The hour came […] in the night rain poured down […]. I looked out on the day. The day was terrible to see. I entered the ship and shut the hatch at once. To have the vessel closed I entrusted the stern and its equipment to Puzur-Amurri [?], the boatman.

Ved daggry rejste en mørk sky sig i horisonten. Inde i den tordnede stormguden Adad. Nabu og Sharru gik i spidsen som herolder over bakker og sletter. Irragal rev fortøjningspælene op. Ninurta slap ødelæggelsen løs. Guderne Anunnaki svang deres fakler og brændte landet med flammerne. Ødelæggelsen fra Adad nåede helt op til himlen. Alt, der havde været lyst, blev mørkt. »Landet som […] i én dag stormen […] blæste voldsomt […] som en kamp […].«
“At daybreak a dark cloud rose on the horizon. Inside it Adad the storm god thundered. Nabu and Sharru went ahead as heralds over hills and plains. Irragal tore up the mooring posts. Ninurta let destruction loose. The Anunnaki gods brandished torches and scorched the land with their flames. The devastation from Adad reached as high as heaven. Everything that had been bright turned dark. ‘The land like […] for one day the storm […] blew fiercely […] like a battle […].’

Bror kunne ikke kende bror. Fra himlen kunne man ikke få øje på et menneske. Selv guderne blev bange for oversvømmelsen. De flygtede op i Anus himmel og satte sig på hug helt ude ved kanten. Som hunde krøb guderne sammen. Ishtar skreg som en fødende kvinde. Gudernes frue, ellers så mild i sin tale, skreg højt: ‘Gid den dag blev til støv, hvor jeg talte så ondt i gudernes forsamling! Hvordan kunne jeg tale så ondt i gudernes forsamling og bestemme, at mit folk skulle udslettes? Har jeg født mit eget folk blot for at mætte havet med deres kroppe som med fiskerogn?’ Guderne, Anunnaki, græd med hende. Ydmygede sad de og græd, med lukkede læber i forsamlingen.
“Brother could not tell brother from brother. From heaven no mortal could be seen. Even the gods were terrified by the flood. They fled up to Anu's heaven and crouched at its far edge. Like dogs the gods cowered. Ishtar screamed like a woman in labour. That gentle-voiced lady of the gods screamed aloud, ‘May the day turn to dust when I spoke such evil in the assembly of the gods! How could I speak such evil in the assembly of the gods and decree the destruction of my people? Did I give birth to my own people only to fill the sea with their bodies like fish spawn?’ The gods, the Anunnaki, wept with her. Humbled, they sat weeping, lips closed in the assembly.

Seks dage og syv nætter fejede orkan, oversvømmelse og storm hen over landet. Da den syvende dag kom, stilnede kampen af. Stormen og oversvømmelsen havde kæmpet som en hær i slagorden. Havet faldt til ro, vinden løjede af, vandet sank. Jeg så ud i dagen. Hver lyd var forstummet. Alle mennesker var blevet til ler igen. Sumpen stod i højde med tagryggen. Jeg åbnede en luge, og sollyset strømmede ned over min kind. Jeg bøjede mig og sad og græd. Tårerne løb ned ad mine kinder. Jeg så ud i det fjerne, helt til havets yderste grænse. Tolv steder kom land til syne. Skibet stødte på grund på bjerget Nisir. Bjerget Nisir holdt det fast og lod det ikke rokke sig. Én dag, så to, holdt Nisir det fast og lod det ikke rokke sig. Tre dage, så fire, holdt Nisir det fast og lod det ikke rokke sig. Fem dage, så seks, holdt Nisir det fast og lod det ikke rokke sig.
“For six days and seven nights hurricane, flood and tempest swept the land. When the seventh day came the battle died down. Storm and flood had fought like an army drawn up for battle. The sea grew calm, the gale was spent, the flood subsided. I looked out on the day. Every sound was stilled. All the people had turned back to clay. The marsh was level with the rooftops. I opened a hatch and sunlight streamed down my cheek. I bowed my head and sat weeping. Tears ran down my cheeks. I looked into the distance, to the furthest edge of the sea. Land rose in twelve places. The ship grounded on Mount Nisir. Mount Nisir held her fast and would not let her stir. One day, then two, Nisir held her fast and would not let her stir. Three days, then four, Nisir held her fast and would not let her stir. Five days, then six, Nisir held her fast and would not let her stir.

Da den syvende dag gryede, tog jeg en due frem og slap den løs. Duen fløj frem og tilbage og kom tilbage til mig. Der var intet sted, den kunne slå sig ned. Så tog jeg en svale frem og slap den løs. Svalen fløj frem og tilbage og kom også tilbage. Der var intet sted, den kunne slå sig ned. Jeg tog en ravn frem og slap den løs. Ravnen fløj ud og så, at vandet var sunket. Den vadede omkring, plaskede og åd. Til mig kom den ikke tilbage.
“When the seventh day dawned I took out a dove and released it. The dove flew back and forth and returned to me. There was nowhere for it to alight. Then I took out a swallow and released it. The swallow flew back and forth and it too returned. There was nowhere for it to alight. I took out a raven and released it. The raven flew out and saw the water had fallen. It waded about, splashed and ate. It did not return to me.

Jeg slap dyrene fri for alle fire vinde. På bjergets top bragte jeg et offer og hældte et drikoffer ud. To gange syv kander stillede jeg frem og dyngede duftende rør, ceder og myrte op under dem. Guderne lugtede duften, guderne lugtede den søde duft. Som fluer samlede guderne sig om ham, der bragte offeret.
“I let the animals go to the four winds. On the mountaintop I offered a sacrifice and poured a libation. I set out twice seven flagons and heaped sweet cane, cedar and myrtle beneath them. The gods smelt the fragrance, the gods smelt the sweet fragrance. Like flies the gods gathered around the one who made the offering.

Gudernes dronning kom. Hun løftede de prægtige smykker, Anu havde ladet lave efter hendes ønske: ‘Guder! Før ville jeg glemme min halskæde af safirer end lade disse dage gå mig af minde. Aldrig vil jeg glemme dem. De andre guder må komme til offeret, men Enlil må ikke. Uden at tænke sig om sendte han oversvømmelsen og overgav mit folk til undergang.’ Så kom Enlil. Ved synet af skibet brød vreden ud i ham. Han rasede mod himmelguderne, Igigi: ‘Er nogen dødelig sluppet væk? Intet menneske skulle kunne overleve ødelæggelsen!’
“The queen of the gods arrived. She raised the magnificent jewels Anu had made to her wishes. ‘Gods! Sooner would I forget my sapphire necklace than let these days slip from my memory. I will never forget them. The other gods may come to the offering, but Enlil may not. Without thought he sent the flood and consigned my people to destruction.’ Then Enlil arrived. At the sight of the ship he burst into anger. He raged at the gods of heaven, the Igigi: ‘Has a mortal escaped? No human should have survived the destruction!’

Ninurta svarede krigeren Enlil: ‘Hvem andre end Ea kunne udtænke sådan en plan? Ea kender jo alle råd.’ Ea svarede krigeren Enlil: ‘Du gudernes anfører, du kriger! Hvordan kunne du uden at spørge nogen til råds sende en oversvømmelse? Lad den, der synder, bære sin synd, og den skyldige sin skyld. Men vis nåde, så han kan leve. Vis barmhjertighed, så han ikke omkommer. I stedet for en oversvømmelse kunne du have sendt en løve for at tage nogle af menneskene. I stedet for en oversvømmelse kunne du have sendt en sjakal for at tage nogle af menneskene. I stedet for en oversvømmelse kunne du have sendt hungersnød for at fortære landet. I stedet for en oversvømmelse kunne du have sendt pestguden for at ramme folket. Jeg røbede ikke de store guders hemmelighed. Jeg lod den mand, der er rig på visdom, drømme en drøm. Sådan fik han gudernes hemmelighed at høre. Overvej nu, hvad der skal ske med ham.’
“Ninurta answered the warrior Enlil, ‘Who but Ea could have devised such a plan? Ea knows every design.’ Ea answered the warrior Enlil, ‘Chief of the gods, warrior! How could you send a flood without taking counsel? Let the sinner bear his sin and the guilty man his guilt. But show mercy so he may live. Be lenient so he won't perish. Instead of a flood you could have sent a lion to reduce humankind. Instead of a flood you could have sent a jackal to reduce humankind. Instead of a flood you could have sent a famine to consume the land. Instead of a flood you could have sent the plague god to overwhelm the people. I did not reveal the great gods' secret. I granted a dream to the man rich in wisdom. That is how he came to hear the gods' secret. Now consider what is to be done with him.’

Da gik Enlil hen til skibet. Han greb mig i hånden og hjalp mig op. Også min kone hjalp han op og lod hende knæle ved min side. Han stod imellem os, rørte ved vores pander og velsignede os: ‘Hidtil har Utnapishtim været et dødeligt menneske. Fra nu af skal Utnapishtim og hans kone være som os guder. Langt borte ved flodernes udmunding skal Utnapishtim bo.’ De tog mig med og lod mig bo langt borte ved flodernes udmunding.
“Then Enlil came up to the ship. He took me by the hand and raised me up. He raised my wife too and had her kneel beside me. Standing between us he touched our foreheads and blessed us. ‘Until now Utnapishtim has been mortal. From now on Utnapishtim and his wife shall be like us gods. Far away at the mouth of the rivers Utnapishtim shall dwell.’ They took me away and settled me far off at the mouth of the rivers.

Men du selv, Gilgamesh, hvem vil samle guderne for din skyld, så du kan finde det liv, du søger? Kom nu, hold dig vågen i seks dage og syv nætter!« Mens Gilgamesh sad på hug, kom søvnen som en brise over ham. Utnapishtim sagde til sin kone: »Se ham, den stærke mand, der beder om liv. Søvnen strøg over ham som en brise!« Hans kone svarede: »Rør ved ham, så han vågner. Lad manden i fred tage hjem ad den vej, han kom, og gå ind i sit land gennem den port, han gik ud ad.«
“But you yourself, Gilgamesh, who will assemble the gods for your sake, so you can find the life you're seeking? Come now, stay awake for six days and seven nights!” As Gilgamesh sat on his haunches sleep came over him like a breeze. Utnapishtim said to his wife, “Look at the strong man who asks for life. Sleep brushed over him like a breeze!” His wife answered, “Touch him and wake him. Let him take the road home in peace, going back to his land through the gate he left by.”

Utnapishtim sagde til sin kone: »Menneskenes bekymringer bekymrer også dig! Bag brød af hans mel og læg det ved hans hoved. Sæt mærker på husmuren for den tid, han sover.« Hun bagte hans brød og lagde det ved hans hoved. På husmuren mærkede hun af, hvor længe han sov. Først blev melet samlet, så blev det sigtet. Tredje gang blev det fugtet, fjerde gang æltede hun dejen, femte gang tilsatte hun surdej, sjette gang blev brødet bagt. Syvende gang rørte han pludselig ved manden, og den stærke mand vågnede.
“Utnapishtim said to his wife, ‘Human troubles trouble you too! Bake bread from his flour and lay it by his head. Mark on the house wall the time he sleeps.’ She baked his bread and laid it by his head. On the house wall she marked how long he slept. First the flour was gathered, next it was sifted. Third it was moistened, fourth she kneaded the dough, fifth she added leaven, sixth it was baked. Seventh he suddenly touched the man, and the strong man woke.”

Gilgamesh sagde til Utnapishtim: »Sig mig, var det dig, der rørte ved mig, lige da søvnen kom over mig, og straks vækkede mig?« Utnapishtim svarede: »Gilgamesh, hvert trin med dit mel blev talt […] først da vækkede jeg dig. Ét: Melet blev samlet. To: Det blev sigtet. Tre: Det blev fugtet. Fire: Hun æltede dejen. Fem: Hun tilsatte surdej. Seks: Det blev bagt. Syv: Jeg rørte pludselig ved dig, og du vågnede.« Gilgamesh svarede: »Hvad skal jeg gøre, og hvor skal jeg gå hen, Utnapishtim? En røver har stjålet mit mod. Døden lurer i mit sovekammer. Hvor jeg end lytter, er der død.«
Gilgamesh said to Utnapishtim, “Tell me, was it you who touched me just as sleep came over me and woke me at once?” Utnapishtim answered, “Gilgamesh, every stage with your flour was counted […] only then did I wake you. One: the flour was gathered. Two: it was sifted. Three: it was moistened. Four: she kneaded the dough. Five: she added leaven. Six: it was baked. Seven: I touched you suddenly and you woke.” Gilgamesh answered, “What shall I do, where shall I go, Utnapishtim? A robber has stolen my courage. Death lurks in my bedroom. Wherever I listen I hear death.”

Utnapishtim sagde til færgemanden Urshanabi: »Urshanabi, […] overfarten vil hade dig. For alle, der kommer til bredden, sætter bredden en grænse. Manden, som du førte herhen, skal snavset blive på hans krop? Skal et skind skjule hans smukke lemmer? Tag ham med til et sted, hvor han kan bade. Lad ham vaske snavset af i vand hvidt som sne og smide sine skind, så havet kan føre dem bort. Lad hans krop vise sin skønhed. Skift båndet om hans pande. Giv ham en kappe, han kan dække sin nøgenhed med, en kappe, der holder sig frisk og ikke ældes, før han er nået hjem til sin by og rejsen er omme.«
Utnapishtim said to the boatman Urshanabi, “Urshanabi, […] the crossing will hate you. For everyone who comes to the shore the shore sets a limit. Is the man you guided here to keep his body covered in dirt? Must a hide conceal the beauty of his limbs? Take him where he can bathe. Have him wash off the dirt in water white as snow and cast off his skins so the sea can carry them away. Let his body show its beauty. Renew the band around his head. Give him a cloak to cover his nakedness, one that will keep its freshness and show no sign of age before he reaches his city and his journey ends.”

Urshanabi førte ham hen, hvor han kunne bade. Han vaskede snavset af i vand hvidt som sne, smed sine skind og lod havet føre dem bort. Hans krop viste sin skønhed. Han skiftede båndet om panden og dækkede sin nøgenhed med en kappe, der skulle holde sig frisk og ikke ældes, før han nåede hjem til sin by og rejsen var omme. Gilgamesh og Urshanabi gik om bord. De satte båden i søen og lagde fra land.
Urshanabi led him to a place to bathe. He washed off the dirt in water white as snow, cast off his skins and let the sea carry them away. His body showed its beauty. He renewed his headband and covered his nakedness with a cloak that would keep its freshness and show no sign of age before he reached his city and his journey ended. Gilgamesh and Urshanabi went aboard. They launched the boat and pushed out onto the waves.

Utnapishtims kone sagde til ham: »Gilgamesh kom hertil slidt op af at ro. Hvad vil du give ham med hjem?« Imens løftede Gilgamesh sin stage og stødte båden fra bredden. Utnapishtim råbte til ham: »Gilgamesh, du kom hertil slidt op af at ro. Hvad skal jeg give dig med hjem til dit land? Jeg vil fortælle dig en hemmelighed. En plante, der ligner en torn, har sin rod dybt nede i havet [?]. Dens torne vil rive dig som en tjørns. Men hvis du når den med hånden, vil du finde livet.« Da Gilgamesh hørte det, løsnede han sit bælte [?] og bandt tunge sten om fødderne. De trak ham ned i havets dyb. Han fandt planten og greb om den. Tornene rev ham. Han skar de tunge sten løs fra fødderne, og havet bragte ham op til bredden igen.
Utnapishtim's wife said to him, “Gilgamesh came here worn out from rowing. What will you give him to take home?” Meanwhile Gilgamesh raised his pole and pushed the boat off the shore. Utnapishtim called to him, “Gilgamesh, you came here worn out from rowing. What shall I give you to take home to your country? I'll tell you a secret. A plant like a thorn has its root deep in the sea [?]. Its prickles will scratch you like a briar. But if your hand reaches it you will find life.” Hearing him, Gilgamesh loosened his belt [?] and bound heavy stones to his feet. They pulled him down into the depths of the sea. He found the plant and seized it. Its thorns scratched him. He cut the heavy stones from his feet and the sea brought him back to shore.

Gilgamesh sagde til færgemanden: »Urshanabi, den plante kan gøre underværker. Med den kan et menneske få sit ønske opfyldt. Jeg tager den med til Uruk bag de høje mure og giver […] af den at spise. Den hedder Gråskæg-bliver-ung-igen. Jeg vil selv spise af den og blive ung igen.«
Gilgamesh said to the boatman, “Urshanabi, this plant can work wonders. With it a man can have his wish. I'll take it to high-walled Uruk and give […] some to eat. Its name is Greybeard-Grows-Young-Again. I'll eat some myself and return to my youth.”

Ved den fyrretyvende time tog de en bid mad, ved den tresindstyvende gjorde de holdt. Gilgamesh fik øje på en dam med køligt vand. Han steg ned i den for at bade. En slange fik færten af planten, skød frem af vandet [?] og snappede den. På vej væk udstødte den en forbandelse [sådan læser 1928-udgaven stedet]. Gilgamesh satte sig ned og brast i gråd. Tårerne løb ned ad hans kinder. Han sagde til færgemanden: »Sig mig, Urshanabi, for hvis skyld har mine arme slidt? For hvis skyld har jeg givet mit hjerteblod? Lønnen blev ikke min. En jordløve fik den løn, jeg vandt. Nu, ved den fyrretyvende time, stjæler den udbyttet. Da jeg åbnede slusen og […] fæstet, så jeg tegnet, der skulle advare mig. Gid jeg var vendt om og havde ladet båden ligge ved havets bred!«
At the fortieth hour they had a bite to eat, at the sixtieth they rested. Gilgamesh noticed a pool of cool water. He went down to bathe. A snake caught the plant's scent, darted up from the water [?] and snatched it. As it drew away it uttered a curse [the 1928 edition's reading]. Gilgamesh sat down and burst into tears. Tears ran down his cheeks. He said to the boatman, “Tell me, Urshanabi, for whose sake have my arms laboured? For whose sake have I spent my heart's blood? The reward wasn't mine. An earth lion got the reward I won. Now, at the fortieth hour, it steals the prize. When I opened the sluice and […] the fitting, I saw the sign sent to warn me. If only I'd turned back and left the boat at the sea's edge!”

Ved den fyrretyvende time tog de en bid mad, ved den tresindstyvende gjorde de holdt. Til sidst nåede de ind i hjertet af Uruk bag de høje mure. Gilgamesh sagde til Urshanabi: »Gå op, Urshanabi, og gå en tur på Uruks mure. Se på fundamentet. Læg mærke til stenene. Er de ikke brændt i ovn? Er grunden ikke lagt med asfalt i syv lag? Én shar er byen, én shar haverne, én shar […] Ishtars tempel. Tre shar og […] har jeg samlet i Uruk.«
At the fortieth hour they had a bite to eat, at the sixtieth they rested. At last they reached the heart of high-walled Uruk. Gilgamesh said to Urshanabi, “Go up, Urshanabi, and walk on the walls of Uruk. Look at the foundations. Take a good look at the bricks. Aren't they kiln-fired? Isn't the groundwork laid with bitumen, seven courses deep? One shar is the city, one shar the gardens, one shar […] Ishtar's temple. Three shar and […] I have brought together in Uruk.”
